          { name: "Treating 60% as Safe and Assuming Homeowner Coalition Stability", tag: "avoid", why: "A 60% result in a high-income suburban district is not a landslide—it is an alignment between the incumbent and an educated, analytically-engaged constituency that evaluates regularly on specific performance metrics. The 40% opposition includes both partisan Republicans and swing voters who are dissatisfied with specific governance choices (tax rates, development policy, school spending efficiency). Any incumbent who treats 60% as a durable floor rather than a coalition to be actively maintained through demonstrated competence and constituent service will find that floor eroding when a serious challenger offers credible alternatives on the specific fiscal and educational concerns that drive this constituency's voting behavior." }
