          { name: "Renter Household Economic Security and Tenant Rights", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 40.9% renter rate, this district has a substantially larger renter population than the national average — a constituency facing housing cost pressure, displacement risk, and tenant protections that are material to their economic security. Renters are historically more fluid in voting behavior than homeowners and more responsive to immediate cost-of-living governance; rising rents or perceived indifference to tenant concerns can shift voting behavior in subsequent cycles. Housing affordability, rent stabilization, and tenant-landlord law are direct governance issues that a representative must engage with specificity and resource commitment, not as secondary concerns." },
          { name: "Multi-Ethnic Coalition Maintenance and Avoiding Demographic Invisibility", tag: "careful", why: "With no demographic group exceeding 40% and four substantial communities (white 39.73%, Black 23.93%, Latino 23.64%, Asian 10.86%), this is a coalition-based district where any constituency group can perceive invisibility if resources and policy attention are disproportionately concentrated. Black and Latino communities in particular have demonstrated sensitivity to perceiving their concerns as deprioritized relative to white professional-class issues; any appearance of governance focused primarily on homeowner, tax, or development concerns while minority community issues remain unaddressed will damage coalition cohesion. Turner must visibly allocate resources, leadership attention, and policy achievement across all four communities." },
          { name: "Treating 73% as a Stable Electoral Floor Rather Than a Coalition Requiring Active Maintenance", tag: "avoid", why: "A 73% margin in a diverse, renter-heavy district is not a durable floor — it is a current result produced by a specific coalition alignment that requires active maintenance. Renters at 40.9%, Black and Latino communities at nearly 48% combined, and professional-class constituencies with distinct policy priorities all require visible constituent service, not passive assumption of loyalty. Turner's predecessor or any challenger could plausibly appeal to any of these groups if they perceive the incumbent as taking them for granted." },
          { name: "Framing Minority Communities as Special Interests Rather Than Core Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where Black and Latino residents combined represent nearly 48% of the population, any framing that positions their concerns — economic development, criminal justice, health equity — as 'special interests' to be accommodated rather than core constituency priorities is both analytically false and politically damaging. These are not interest groups competing with 'regular' constituents; they are nearly half the district. Any rhetorical or policy move that positions them outside the mainstream constituency will directly alienate a plurality of the district's voters." }
