          "The 64% Democratic margin reflects this constituency's alignment with progressive education investment, social-liberal positioning on cultural issues, and tax-policy frameworks that protect professional-class asset values. But the margin is not as stable as it appears. New Jersey's property tax system creates acute financial pressure on high-income homeowners: residents at $125K+ income own homes in high-property-value markets and face effective tax rates among the nation's highest. The renter rate of 32.5% means two-thirds of households have direct property tax exposure. A Democratic incumbent who is perceived as indifferent to property tax burden, unable to deliver equitable school funding, or dismissive of professional-class economic concerns will find this margin evaporates quickly — not toward Republicans necessarily, but toward primary challengers or low turnout. The 64% is a mandate to govern with attention to property tax reform, school-quality accountability, and the specific concerns of a constituency that has voted Democratic but retains the economic sophistication to demand results."
