          { name: "College-Educated Voter Expectations for Competent Governance", tag: "careful", why: "The 37.7% college attainment rate and $100,420 income create a constituency that evaluates candidates on policy specificity, fiscal responsibility, and whether government actually delivers — not partisan loyalty or abstract ideological positioning. The 34% Republican opposition includes educated voters who may be persuadable on specific issues if the incumbent is perceived as incompetent, wasteful, or ideologically extreme; losing the margin among this cohort would require an incumbent to perform substantially below the 66% baseline. This is a district where implementation matters as much as intention, and where credible opposition can exploit governance failures or perceived ineffectuality." },
          { name: "Partisan Messaging That Neglects Material Constituent Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "NJ-SD-22's 66% Democratic margin can create a false sense of security that allows an incumbent to rely on partisan messaging rather than constituent service; this error is particularly costly in a high-income, educated district where voters have resources to evaluate performance independently. Generic Democratic talking points will underperform with both the core Latino and Black coalition — who need proof of healthcare and housing delivery — and with educated swing voters who expect sophistication and specificity. An incumbent who campaigns on partisan identity rather than on what he has delivered for housing, healthcare, and economic mobility will find the margin eroding in a district this affluent." },
          { name: "Treating 66% as Mandate Rather Than Coalition Requiring Active Maintenance", tag: "avoid", why: "A 66% result is a strong position but not a landslide so large that coalition maintenance becomes optional; the renter supermajority and the dependence on Latino and Black voters mean the coalition is held by active delivery and constituent attention, not by demographic inheritance. Any incumbent who treats the 66% as a stable floor and neglects the material concerns of renters, communities of color, and younger voters will find that coalition fragmenting in a higher-turnout cycle or when a credible alternative emerges. In a district where Democrats have built a real majority, the obligation is to maintain it through governance, not to assume it is permanent." }
