          "The district's demographic composition — 49.5% white, 22.6% Hispanic, 12.7% Asian, 11.2% Black, with roughly 4% unaccounted for — reveals a genuinely diverse affluent suburb where multiple communities have achieved or are achieving upper-middle-class status simultaneously. This is not a district where one demographic group dominates the political economy; it is a place where educated professionals across racial and ethnic lines have converged, and where maintaining that economic stability across all communities is a shared material interest. The 22.6% Hispanic population and 12.7% Asian population are substantially higher than state averages, and both communities in high-income suburban contexts tend to be highly engaged voters with specific policy concerns around education quality, property tax fairness, and economic mobility for their children. Any candidate — particularly a challenger seeking to crack the 71% Democratic baseline — must understand that this is not a district where appeals to white working-class economic anxiety or racial backlash will gain traction; it is a place where affluent, educated voters of multiple backgrounds are evaluating whether their taxes are spent wisely, whether schools are excellent, and whether the community remains safe and stable. The stability of McKeon's margin depends on whether these communities believe those conditions are being met."
