          "The district's affluence and educational attainment create a constituency that votes on specific material interests: property tax burden, school funding sufficiency, infrastructure quality, and whether public services justify the cost of living in a high-income New Jersey community. At $94,652 median income with three-quarters homeownership, the primary economic anxiety is not scarcity but value-for-money — whether the property taxes and cost of living in this area deliver proportional public services and quality of life. The 2025 Democratic sweep suggests either that Democratic messaging on these material concerns resonated convincingly, or that Republican candidates failed to offer a credible counter-narrative on tax burden, school governance, or service delivery. For any candidate seeking to hold or challenge this seat, the strategic leverage lies in: (1) for Democrats, maintaining turnout among a constituency that could become complacent if the margin appears safe; (2) for Republicans, identifying specific grievances on property tax, school governance, or service quality where the sitting majority's record is vulnerable, and building a credible case that Republican governance offers better value — not lower taxes in the abstract, but better-managed public money."
