          { name: "Aging, Affluent Homeowner Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "NJ-AD-9 is the oldest district in the dataset with a 54.6-year median age and 87.3% homeownership, paired with an $81,889 median household income and 30.1% college attainment — a profile of established, asset-holding households facing rising fixed costs on relatively stable incomes. Property tax management, healthcare cost containment, and retirement security are the primary economic concerns for this constituency. The Republican sweep reflects alignment with this homeowner majority, but the incoming representative must actively address the material anxieties — property tax burden, local service costs, healthcare inflation — that keep this constituency engaged rather than assuming the 100% margin represents passive support." },
          { name: "Small Hispanic Community Integration & Economic Participation", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 8.27% of the population, NJ-AD-9 has a meaningful Hispanic constituency that exists in a district dominated by white homeowner concerns and has received minimal specific policy attention in the 100% Republican sweep. The district's $81,889 income and 87.3% homeownership suggest that Hispanic households here may be concentrated in service-sector and construction work — occupations directly affected by labor market conditions, housing affordability, and access to reliable healthcare. A representative who addresses Hispanic community economic participation and integration with specific policy — rather than treating the 8.27% as a footnote to the white majority — builds credibility and prevents this constituency from becoming available to a challenger in a future cycle." },
          { name: "The Meaning and Stability of a 100% Republican Sweep", tag: "careful", why: "A 2-seat at-large district that sweeps 100% for one party across both seats is extremely rare in competitive states and warrants analytical scrutiny beyond the raw margin. The 54.6-year median age, 87.3% homeownership, $81,889 income, and 84.72% white population create strong structural Republican alignment, but the complete absence of any Democratic representation across two seats suggests either exceptional Republican candidate quality, exceptional Democratic weakness in field and messaging, or both. A new representative should treat the 100% result as a strategic asset to be actively defended rather than a stable floor — the same demographic profile that produced this result could, with different candidates or messaging, produce a more competitive outcome." },
          { name: "Dismissing the 8.27% Hispanic Community as Peripheral", tag: "avoid", why: "A district where the representative consolidates the white homeowner 84.72% majority and treats the Hispanic 8.27% as a solved problem or a special interest demanding accommodation will lose credibility with that community and create an opening for a challenger who engages Hispanic constituent concerns directly. In a 100% Republican sweep, there is no partisan competitor currently challenging on these issues — but absence of competition does not mean absence of constituent need." },
          { name: "Taking Aging Homeowner Economic Anxiety as Solved by Party Affiliation", tag: "avoid", why: "The Republican sweep reflects structural alignment with this older, affluent, homeowner-heavy constituency, but the real drivers of support are solutions to specific economic concerns — property tax burden, local service delivery, healthcare cost management — not abstract party loyalty. A representative who assumes that 100% Republican registration means 100% certainty on governance performance will miss the opportunity to build durable constituent relationships around the material issues that matter most to a 54.6-year-old, $81,889-income, 87.3% homeowner constituency." }
