          { name: "Treating 100% as a Durable Majority", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% sweep in a 2-seat at-large district with 44.6% college attainment and $109,498 median income is not evidence of deep partisan realignment — it is a single-cycle result that reflects the specific choices of voters in a particular moment. Affluent, educated suburban voters are precisely the swing constituency that shifts between parties based on performance, competence, and material interests. Any Republican incumbent who treats this 100% result as a stable floor rather than a vulnerable ceiling will be unprepared when a credible Democratic challenger emerges with a message about fiscal stewardship, education quality, or targeted attention to Hispanic and Asian professional communities." },
          { name: "Democratic Mobilization as a Permanent Collapse", tag: "avoid", why: "The 0% Democratic result does not mean Democratic voters have left the district permanently — it means they either did not turn out in sufficient numbers, or the 2025 Democratic candidates and message failed to make a credible case for why affluent, educated professionals should support them. In a district where median income is $109,498 and college attainment is 44.6%, the Democratic opportunity lies in persuasion around competence, education quality, and fiscal management, not in mobilizing a permanently lost base. A well-resourced, competent Democratic challenger offering a substantive alternative on these issues can reorganize substantial portions of this constituency." }
