          { name: "Hispanic Community Economic Integration", tag: "careful", why: "At 21.07%, the Hispanic population is substantial and likely concentrated in service, construction, and healthcare sectors that support the affluent homeowner majority. This community's economic mobility, workplace rights, and access to professional credential pathways are distinct from the Anglo homeowner narrative but essential to the district's functional economy. A candidate who engages the Hispanic community on concrete economic opportunity — not just symbolic inclusion — addresses a constituency whose labor is material to the district's functioning but whose political voice can be dormant if not actively cultivated." },
          { name: "Treating a 50-50 Split as a Settled Outcome", tag: "avoid", why: "The district is literally split 1 Democratic, 1 Republican — making this the definition of a competitive seat in which 50% of voters actively chose the opposition in the most recent cycle. Any candidate who campaigns this district as though their party has a reliable base or natural coalition is misreading the fundamental arithmetic. A 50-50 split in an affluent, educated district means swing voters dominate the electorate and messaging must address their specific concerns, not consolidate partisan loyalty." },
          { name: "Partisan Culture War Framing in an Affluent, Professional District", tag: "avoid", why: "An affluent, 53% college-educated district with $129,611 median income evaluates candidates primarily on economic competence, fiduciary responsibility, and whether government delivers tangible results — not on cultural positioning. Heavy investment in culture-war messaging or appeals to partisan identity in a district where half the voters already chose the other party is strategically counterproductive. The swing voters here respond to concrete governance answers about taxes, schools, and economic competitiveness, not abstract cultural claims." }
