          { name: "Assuming White Voter Consolidation or Alienating White Professionals on Class-Based Issues", tag: "avoid", why: "At 33.95%, white voters are now a plurality rather than a majority in NJ-37, yet they remain one-third of the population and include affluent, college-educated professionals whose support contributed to the Democratic sweep—potentially through policy alignment on education, climate, or institutional competence rather than through racial bloc voting. An incumbent who frames the district primarily around racial and ethnic diversity while neglecting the economic concerns (housing affordability, tax fairness, service quality) that affect white professional households across all income levels risks fragmenting a coalition that may depend on cross-racial, class-based alignment. The median age of 42.6 suggests a constituency of established professionals and families—both white and nonwhite—with shared concerns about housing costs, education quality, and economic security that deserve integrated policy attention rather than siloed community engagement." }
